anytime you don't have to ask permission  is awesome and now you don't I grew up  on comic books my father taught me to  read with comic books when I was a  little kid spider-man the Hulk or on TV  it's always been a part of how I see the  world Jason McNamara is an award-winning  graphic novelist based in Northern  California he's been creating and  publishing comics for the better part of  a decade but when it came time to issue  his latest creation Jason decided to  bypass the traditional publishing route  largely controlled by diamond comic  distributors and go online instead so  the Rattler is a book that I wrote  co-created with Greg Hinkle was an  artist in LA and we spent a long time on  the book it wasn't convinced that going  with a publisher or going with diamond  was the best first step for the book I  thought we could get more attention and  awareness of the project if we did a  very public Kickstarter part of what i  like about Kickstarter is that you're  selling the story of its creation you're  not just selling the book Jason is just  one of the many in the comics community  that's embracing digital platforms like  Kickstarter and comiXology to finance  and publish their projects the emergence  of these digital platforms has provided  a more diverse selection of comics for  fans by allowing creators to break  through editorial gatekeepers and  produce content without sacrificing  artistic integrity or ownership Matt  sylheti chair of the comics program at  california college of the arts describes  the traditional publishing process  traditionally publishers were sort of a  the gatekeepers of comics and that's not  necessarily a terrible thing I mean  there's an editorial I that sort of  curates the stuff that gets out into  bookshelves but other than that you  basically were folding and stapling  comics by yourselves and trying to hand  sell them at shows maybe a publisher  took a look at your stuff and decided  they want to collect it but most of the  cartoonists and comics writers that I  know had to do it themselves though most  comic creators do a bulk of publishing  work themselves they need a distributor  to get their books onto shelves and into  the hands of customers let's say that  you've got a great book  you even had the funds to do a print run  yourself there's still the hurdle of  getting your books in the comic shops in  order to get in the comic shop so you  had to go through diamond they do have a  monopoly on sort of the comics community  and diamond does a lot of great things  for retailing but they also have a  business model that requires you to have  a certain number of pre-orders for you  even to be sort of considered as part of  the catalog and if you're not in that  catalog nobody's seeing your work but  now you have these emerging platforms  like comiXology which has a lower  barrier of entry they accept more books  so diamond will turn a lot of stuff down  based on whether they think it's worth  it to them if its content you know you  have to appeal to their taste on what  they think the comiXology is more open  so why ask permission you don't have to  ask permission you can just do it  instead of asking a publisher for  permission Jason turned to his fans and  launched a Kickstarter to raise funds to  print his own book while he initially  sought forty six hundred dollars in  backing he brought in over three times  his goal so by going through Kickstarter  first we could get a real idea of what  the interest is for this book and I do  think that people that buy books from  Kickstarter are not the same people that  buy books in the store there is an  element of of entrepreneurship and  there's a gamble that appeals to people  in kickstarter I've had better  conversations with people that I've had  over the past ten years of doing  conventions like I can meet them here I  can meet their expectations that I can  engage them in a way I can't at any  other venue as more creators are turning  to online platforms to publish their  work consumers are also reacting to the  digital marketplace favorably comic  sales broke industry records in 2014  with digital comics contributing an  estimated 90 million dollars in sales  the rise of these digital platforms has  only helped to expand readership which  has led to bigger profits in the comics  industry and an increase of brick and  mortar retailers I don't think digital  digital's going to take over but i do  think digital and and the retailers will  strike a balance the books that i buy  now from retailers are usually higher  end products like hundred-dollar artist  collections from idw i don't buy as many  floppy and stuff but i spend more money  and on retailers on higher end stuff  that you can't get in the same digital  experience so what does this all mean  for the future of the comics industry  overall comics is healthier than ever  but if you look at the sort of projects  that are out there in the web comics  world that are now trickling into the  Kickstarter comics world the diversity  is amazing and not just in terms of the  types of projects in the genres but in  terms of the creators and if the book is  good enough it will find an audience and  if it didn't like you learned your  lessons and you'll do something  different next time but you don't have  to make content for them you can make  content for yourself at any time you  don't have to ask permission is awesome  and now you don't so publish it or don't  click the links below to see more and  subscribe to our channel to get all the  latest videos from reason TV
 

